96 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133996 #edb/ref 1045 "The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133996 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133996 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133996 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133996 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e1b56295-70ee-55b2-8a65-97f8ae733716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134760 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134760 #edb/ref 1061 26178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134760 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e063a5e6-8819-5814-8c62-dae890a57d71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134760 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134824 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134824 #edb/ref 1061 32622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134824 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a10e6c95-833b-528d-a1f9-411415a6a652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134824 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134825 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134825 #edb/ref 1061 32627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134825 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f869fbb2-2435-5488-b530-01a592efdf62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134825 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134850 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134850 #edb/ref 1061 35588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134850 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "879e7253-5b1c-559d-8414-14057286be07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134850 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134860 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134860 #edb/ref 1061 35721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134860 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15f14408-48b8-5f0f-8c55-d5009dc56e57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134860 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134861 #edb/ref 1061 35722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134861 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48691aa9-a37a-52cd-a44c-e9ef2b10ea85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134861 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135084 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b].$ The fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x = f(b) - f(a).\n$$\n\nA similar result, known as **the fundamental theorem for line integrals,** exists for line integrals of conservative vector fields. This theorem can be stated as follows:\n\n*Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ Then,*\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll discuss how this theorem is proved in simple cases at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's take a look at a concrete example of how to apply this theorem." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135084 #edb/ref 1065 4306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135084 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0c3e4762-a85a-5881-81ad-857e0bec5a48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135084 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135127 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y) = x^2y^3$ and $C$ is the path along the curve $y=x^2$ for $x \\in [0,2].$ \n\nFirst, we must parameterize the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t^2\\right\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of $C$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(0)\t= \\left\\langle 0, 0\\right\\rangle, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{r}(2)\t= \\left\\langle 2, 4\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the path's endpoints defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,4)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3}-{0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =256.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice how much faster it is to evaluate a line integral when we can use the fundamental theorem." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135127 #edb/ref 1065 5829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135127 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8ce39e74-c98d-54af-8d93-20f6b5bca39a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135127 #edb/ref 1067 "A Concrete Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135128 #edb/ref 1064 "Here, we prove the fundamental theorem of line integrals in the two-dimensional case. The three-dimensional case is similar.\n\nLet $C$ be a smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function of two variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ We wish to show that\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nUsing our usual formula for calculating line integrals, we can write the left-hand side as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}∇f\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the integrand above is simply the derivative of $f(\\mathbf r(t))$ with respect to $t.$ Thus, we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}(f(\\mathbf r(t)))\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the fundamental theorem of calculus, this is equal to \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nThis concludes the proof in the case where $C$ is smooth. It's easy to show that the theorem also holds for piecewise smooth curves." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135128 #edb/ref 1065 5830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135128 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0ef0926c-7a69-5fc8-91f0-7f40e3be9be9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135128 #edb/ref 1067 "Proof of the Fundamental Theorem for Smooth Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238279 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pi -1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238279 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238279 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1adb26c2-291f-5ecb-be27-a83497de5b74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238492 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{2}-e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238492 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238492 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "324fc058-3716-52e2-915c-a8ff8548ce66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238616 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238616 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238616 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40f10544-0cd1-50c6-9897-c035b59965c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239384 #edb/ref 1080 "1+e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239384 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239384 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "99009bfa-183e-50ad-b31d-4a8cd8b1b4fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239482 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239482 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239482 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a4933581-9ab8-5174-ab43-a4a7af154ea9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239563 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239563 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239563 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "add0b6a3-0052-52a8-a4b1-57278ac77df3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239575 #edb/ref 1080 "e-{e}^{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239575 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239575 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ae9e232f-a132-5ae1-a985-ee43b1e859ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239588 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pi +1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239588 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239588 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b0815d5d-2eab-5eb6-b0b6-cea74e67c474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239632 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239632 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239632 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b4578f1a-d647-5132-b9c6-b097afd1fc07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239695 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239695 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239695 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc186f6a-3c28-5f17-b90f-9614a603b361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240344 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240344 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240344 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240344 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240344 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240344 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240344 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240344 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186239632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240344 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0bc00398-8a1c-5911-a2b1-71601fb149ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240454 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240454 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240454 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186239482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240454 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "46f03b38-7ac7-5b2a-bebf-d97ba46b022c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240905 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240905 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240905 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240905 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240905 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b025c1d-a393-5ea5-8c4e-6e0786f6f25d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240905 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ for $x\\in [1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45e3e133-a99c-58ca-b74f-4cbfe235bb72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240907 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256839 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{6}-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256839 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256839 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06c9589f-c628-5f4a-a78a-70eef556a95c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256925 #edb/ref 1080 "3e+1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256925 #ed