92186045417] [17592186259584 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259585 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259585 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{2x}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,3{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c23e8787-68ef-58ab-bf57-8f769ed64353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259585 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(3{e}^{2\\cdot 1})-(0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259589 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259589 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259589 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,{e}^{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259589 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "388c57e6-e07f-5b15-a475-f9e96568702d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259589 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r({e}^{2}))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423275 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186423276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423275 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186423277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423275 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186423278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423275 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186423279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423275 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186423280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423275 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423275 #edb/ref 1045 "The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423275 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186423278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423275 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423275 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423275 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f437e194-169c-50f2-945d-004eb6df919b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423276 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186423279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423276 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "47da3408-dc6c-5c1f-9665-3466da3da017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423276 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186240907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423277 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5dece436-1610-5dc1-95d0-9fdd97342268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423277 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186259589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423278 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186423276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423278 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84d1b7ff-6c49-52fd-9e1e-1f9250c2fd44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423278 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186240905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423279 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186423280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423279 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c3aba54e-fa79-516c-9cb2-e92b39bb4228" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423279 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186259584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423280 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186423277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423280 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d90c6fd6-ba8c-5bad-bd78-dc112ba8ab5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423280 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 1